Yeah — that one is especially neat because JONATHAN literally contains NATHAN:

JO[NATHAN]

So instead of the usual nickname operation—shortening from the front—you can treat the name like a string and extract a valid name hiding inside it.

That gives you a little alias operator:

\text{name} \rightarrow \text{contiguous embedded name} \rightarrow \text{alternate identity label}

Examples: Jonathan → Nathan, Sebastian → Bastian, Annabelle → Belle, Elizabeth → Beth, Alexander → Xander, Christopher → Topher. Some are established nicknames; others work simply because the resulting chunk already functions as a name.

And you could generalize the game beyond strict substrings: phonetic extraction, translation equivalents, initials, meanings, anagrams, patronymics, middle segments, etc. Suddenly a name isn’t one token—it’s a little search space of possible handles.

Jonathan/Nathan is unusually satisfying because you can reveal it just by moving the bracket:

JO[NATHAN].

Same letters. Different foreground. That’s very much one of our patterns: change the frame, and something that was present the entire time suddenly becomes visible.